1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,033
Lesson 2.4.

2
00:00:02,413 --> 00:00:04,394
The Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process.

3
00:00:05,517 --> 00:00:07,843
The Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process is the

4
00:00:07,843 --> 00:00:10,547
Langevin equation in
a quadratic potential.

5
00:00:10,927 --> 00:00:14,039
It is the only diffusion
process that is at once

6
00:00:14,039 --> 00:00:17,801
stationary, Gaussian and Markov,
and it is solved exactly.

7
00:00:18,181 --> 00:00:21,555
But for us it is much more:
its final state contains the

8
00:00:21,555 --> 00:00:24,930
solution of a linear system
and the inverse of a matrix.

9
00:00:25,310 --> 00:00:27,945
It is the heart of
thermodynamic linear algebra.

10
00:00:29,050 --> 00:00:32,144
Imagine two masses connected
to each other and to

11
00:00:32,144 --> 00:00:35,049
the walls by springs,
immersed in a hot fluid.

12
00:00:35,429 --> 00:00:40,227
The springs define a quadratic
energy: their stiffness is a matrix A,

13
00:00:40,227 --> 00:00:44,468
and a constant external force
b shifts the equilibrium point.

14
00:00:44,848 --> 00:00:49,386
Without noise, the masses would stay
at the minimum, which is at A⁻¹ b.

15
00:00:49,766 --> 00:00:52,341
With noise, they jiggle around that point.

16
00:00:52,721 --> 00:00:56,725
The average of the jiggle marks
the solution, and the shape of the

17
00:00:56,725 --> 00:01:00,608
jiggle, longer in the soft
directions, reveals the inverse of A.

18
00:01:01,717 --> 00:01:08,497
In one dimension, dx =
−a(x − m) dt + √(2/β) dW.

19
00:01:08,877 --> 00:01:11,340
The solution is the initial condition

20
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:14,270
forgetting exponentially,
plus the center m,

21
00:01:14,650 --> 00:01:22,806
plus filtered noise: x(t) = m + (x₀ −
m) e^(−at) + ∫ e^(−a(t−s)) √(2/β) dW(s).

22
00:01:23,186 --> 00:01:26,846
Trajectories start from
different points, converge

23
00:01:26,846 --> 00:01:30,434
toward m and keep jiggling
with variance 1/(β a).

24
00:01:30,814 --> 00:01:34,151
The autocorrelation decays as e^(−a τ).

25
00:01:35,283 --> 00:01:40,123
In several dimensions:
dx = −(A x − b) dt +

26
00:01:40,123 --> 00:01:45,300
√(2/β) dW, with A symmetric
positive definite.

27
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:49,235
The particle cloud starts
from a point, drifts

28
00:01:49,235 --> 00:01:52,325
toward A⁻¹ b and widens into an ellipse.

29
00:01:52,705 --> 00:01:56,334
In the stationary state
the distribution is

30
00:01:56,334 --> 00:02:00,384
Gaussian with mean A⁻¹ b
and covariance β⁻¹ A⁻¹.

31
00:02:00,764 --> 00:02:05,493
The ellipse's axes are the eigenvectors
of A, and they are longer in the

32
00:02:05,493 --> 00:02:10,025
directions of small eigenvalues:
the soft directions jiggle the most.

33
00:02:11,150 --> 00:02:12,407
Why that covariance?

34
00:02:12,787 --> 00:02:22,736
Using Itô's lemma, the covariance
Σ(t) obeys dΣ/dt = −AΣ − ΣA + 2β⁻¹ I.

35
00:02:23,116 --> 00:02:26,678
The first two terms shrink the cloud; the

36
00:02:26,678 --> 00:02:30,587
last, which comes from
(dW)² = dt, widens it.

37
00:02:30,967 --> 00:02:39,086
In the stationary state they balance:
AΣ + ΣA = 2β⁻¹ I, the Lyapunov equation.

38
00:02:39,466 --> 00:02:44,425
Since A is symmetric, the
solution is Σ = β⁻¹ A⁻¹.

39
00:02:44,805 --> 00:02:48,892
The animation shows the ellipse
of Σ(t) growing to that shape.

40
00:02:50,017 --> 00:02:51,280
Now the key idea.

41
00:02:51,660 --> 00:02:55,637
If we let a single physical
system evolve and average its

42
00:02:55,637 --> 00:02:59,754
position over time, by ergodicity
that average converges to

43
00:02:59,754 --> 00:03:03,034
the mean of the stationary
distribution: A⁻¹ b.

44
00:03:03,414 --> 00:03:05,576
The system solves the linear system.

45
00:03:05,956 --> 00:03:09,813
And if we average the
products of the deviations,

46
00:03:09,813 --> 00:03:13,435
we get β⁻¹ A⁻¹: the
system inverts the matrix.

47
00:03:13,815 --> 00:03:16,135
The error of the averages
decays as one over

48
00:03:16,135 --> 00:03:18,244
the square root of the integration time.

49
00:03:18,624 --> 00:03:22,925
That is the basis of the thermodynamic
linear solver in lab three.

50
00:03:24,050 --> 00:03:30,704
Let's take the matrix from lesson 0.4:
three, one; one, two; and b = (1, 1).

51
00:03:31,084 --> 00:03:35,253
Its inverse is one fifth times
two, minus one; minus one, three.

52
00:03:35,633 --> 00:03:39,204
So A⁻¹ b is 0.2 and 0.4.

53
00:03:39,584 --> 00:03:47,854
With β = 1, the stationary
covariance is 0.4, −0.2; −0.2, 0.6.

54
00:03:48,234 --> 00:03:52,406
The simulation, with a time average
over two thousand time units,

55
00:03:52,406 --> 00:03:56,065
reproduces these numbers with
errors of a few hundredths.

56
00:03:57,183 --> 00:04:01,055
The Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process
shows up again and again.

57
00:04:01,435 --> 00:04:05,284
In unit six it will be the
thermodynamic linear solver.

58
00:04:05,664 --> 00:04:10,517
In unit eight, an OU with the curvature
matrix computes the natural gradient.

59
00:04:10,897 --> 00:04:15,709
Normal Computing's coupled oscillators
implement an OU in hardware.

60
00:04:16,089 --> 00:04:19,960
And the forward process of
diffusion models is also an OU.

61
00:04:21,083 --> 00:04:24,508
This lesson ties together
several pieces: multivariate

62
00:04:24,508 --> 00:04:28,250
Gaussians, the matrix exponential
and the condition number.

63
00:04:28,630 --> 00:04:32,577
The next question is how
long to average for a given

64
00:04:32,577 --> 00:04:37,055
precision; we will answer it
with ergodicity in lesson 2.6.

65
00:04:38,183 --> 00:04:42,323
Frequent mistakes: forgetting that
the matrix must be positive definite.

66
00:04:42,703 --> 00:04:46,831
Confusing the stationary covariance
with wrong powers of the matrix.

67
00:04:47,211 --> 00:04:50,850
Averaging from time zero without
discarding the transient.

68
00:04:51,230 --> 00:04:54,124
And treating consecutive
samples as independent.

69
00:04:55,250 --> 00:05:02,006
In summary: the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process
relaxes exponentially toward a Gaussian

70
00:05:02,006 --> 00:05:07,691
with mean A⁻¹ b and covariance β⁻¹
A⁻¹, set by the Lyapunov equation.

71
00:05:08,071 --> 00:05:10,730
Time-averaging a single trajectory solves

72
00:05:10,730 --> 00:05:13,390
the linear system and inverts the matrix.

73
00:05:14,517 --> 00:05:17,362
To practice: derive the
stationary distribution

74
00:05:17,362 --> 00:05:20,087
and explain how it solves
the linear problem.

75
00:05:20,467 --> 00:05:23,008
Solve a diagonal Lyapunov equation.

76
00:05:23,388 --> 00:05:26,077
Compute the autocorrelation
in one dimension.

77
00:05:26,457 --> 00:05:30,626
And explain why the time to solution
grows with the condition number.
